      "text": "Let $x = \\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}$.\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\nx + \\sqrt{3}\n\t&= \\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} \\\\\n\t&= \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ + \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} \\\\\n\t&= \\frac{2 \\cos \\left(80^\\circ + 30^\\circ\\right)}{\\sin 160^\\circ} \\\\\n\t&= \\frac{2 \\cos 110^\\circ}{- \\sin 40^\\circ} \\\\\n\t&= \\frac{2 \\sin 70^\\circ}{8^{-1} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ} \\\\\n\t&= 16.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\\cos 80^\\circ + \\sqrt{3} \\sin 80^\\circ = \\boxed{16}.\\]Comment:\nI skipped some steps in the expansion and introduction of the product-to-sum and sum-to-product identities to prove that two products of $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are equal to each other.",
